  "boxes": [
    {
      "type": "anchorbox",
      "title": "The Universal Scholar",
      "description": "Sages, gods, and cosmic figures (Sanaka, Brahma, Narada, Sarasvati, and Shesha) praise Hanuman's intellectual greatness. In the *Valmiki Ramayana* (*Kishkindha Kanda, 3:28-32*), Rama marvels at Hanuman's flawless speech and mastery of the Vedas and grammar, stating that even a king with such a brilliant advisor/envoy succeeds in all his endeavors.",
      "motivating_text": "",
      "citation": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "gotchabox",
      "items": [
        "The श/ष → स Shift in Action: Beautiful symmetry here! Sanskrit मुनीश (Muneesha), शारदा (Shaarada), and अहीश (Aheesha) drop their heavy palatal श (sh) and soften into the pure Awadhi rhymes: मुनीसा (Muneesaa), सारद (Saarad), and अहीसा (Aheesaa).",
        "The 'Aadi' (आदि) suffix: The suffix आदि (ādi) is used to denote \"and others like them\" or \"starting from\". \nThis chaupai starts a list of greats, but we will know about why they are mentioned here in the next chaupai."
      ]
    }
  ]
